.contents SMALL
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
.foil Introduction
.b CCLVM1 is a museum-quality VM/CMS Environment hosted in the
Classical Computing Laboratory at IBM Poughkeepsie
.d An employee-run historical IT center on the IBM campus in
Poughkeepsie, NY
.d Open to all IBM employees and official site visitors by appointment
.d Currently undergoing renovations, expected reopening in 2028
.b Accurately recreates the kind of timesharing environments used at
IBM and other companies from the 1970s through the 1990s
.*----------------------------
.foil Hardware Overview
.b IBM 3006-B01 System/390 Integrated server
.b IBM 3174-21L Establishment Controller (channel-attached)
.b IBM 3278-2 Display Station
.b IBM 3278-5 Display Station
.b IBM 3279-S2B Color Display Station
.b IBM 3279-S3G Color Display Station
.b IBM 3299 8-Port Coax Multiplexor
.b IBM 4224-1 Dot Matrix Line Printer
.*----------------------------
.foil IBM 3006-B01 System/390 Integrated Server
.b Introduced on November 12, 1998
.b Enterprise-grade Pentium II server with mainframe co-processor
.b Based off of "Personal System/370" and "Personal System/390" lines
of mainframe-compatible PC workstations
.b Has an OS/2 host operating system on the Pentium II, running S/390
operating systems in parallel on the mainframe co-processor
.d OS/2 host conceptually serves the same function as the S/390 Support
Element, just with a completely different software stack
.b Allowed for mainframe functionality on real hardware, without the
need for expensive additional S/390 devices such as 3480/90 tape drives
or 3390 disk storage
.b Succeeded by the IBM 7060 Multiprise 3000, a real mainframe in same
form-factor case
.*----------------------------
.foil IBM 3174-21L Establishment Controller
.b Introduced in 1986
.b Needed to connect terminals and printers to mainframes
.b Processes I/O operations for the terminals and printers
.b Ours is a rack-mount model that connects with System/360-style "bus
and tag" channel cables
.d Large floor models and small desktop models were also available
.b The 3174 could be expended with a range of options and adapters:
channel, ESCON, serial (V.24/V.35), coax, fiber optic "coax", ISDN,
token-ring, ethernet
.*----------------------------
.foil IBM 3278 Display Station
.b Monochrome mainframe terminal introduced in 1977
.b Introduced the following features:
.d Extended Highlighting: ability to set highlighting attributes on
individual characters as well as on fields (blinking, character set,
reverse video, and underscored)
.d Accented characters and dead keys
.d Programmed Symbols: programmable characters, able to display
monochrome graphics (with 3620 Character Set Extension and 5790
Programmed Symbols)
.b CCLVM1 features two models:
.d Model 2: 80 x 24 character resolution
.d Model 5: 132 x 27 character resolution, switchable to 80 x 24
.*----------------------------
.foil IBM 3279 Display Station
.b First IBM color terminal, introduced in 1979
.b Uses same "Extended Data Stream" as the 3278
.d Think of it as a 3278 with color
.b Two color sets available:
.d Base color: Greed, Red, Blue, White
.d Extended color: Base colors plus Pink, Yellow, and Turquoise
.b CCLVM1 features two models:
.d Model S2B: 80 x 24 character resolution with extended color
.d Model S3G: 80 x 32 character resolution with extended color and
programmed symbol set graphics
.*----------------------------
.foil IBM 4224-1 Dot Matrix Line Printer
.b Low-end enterprise line printer from 1986
.b Speed of around 200 characters-per-second in draft mode
.d Compare with high-end 6262-D22 of same era: 2,200 lines per minute!
.b Several variants were available:
.d System/36, System/38, and AS/400 (twainax interface)
.d System/370 and System/390 (coax interface)
.d Series/1 (parallel ASCII interface)
.d System/88 (serial ASCII interface)
.*----------------------------
.foil VM/CMS Operating Environment
.b VM is a :hp3.hypervisor:ehp3., an operating system that serves
virtual machines
.d The current version, z/VM, is used to host large Linux server farms
.b Introduced internally in 1967 as :hp2.CP-67:ehp2., first sold to the
public as :hp2.VM/370:ehp2. in 1972.
.b Was conceived as a solution to the :hp3.time-sharing problem:ehp3.,
that is, the problem of allowing more than one person to use a computer
simultaneously
.d Each user would get their own virtual machine, which could run any
System/360 or System/370 operating system
.d General purpose users used :hp2.CMS:ehp2., a single-tasking
operating system with a non-hierarchical filesystem
.d Other popular uses were for operating system development, and for
partitioning a System/370 for running multiple instances of VSE
.*----------------------------
.foilcont
.b By the 1980s, IBM had extensive internal infrastructure built
around VM and CMS
.d In a world before PCs, IBMers used CMS virtual machines for tasks
like email, calendaring, document preparation, application development,
and end-user applications
.d A global CMS-based bulletin board system served as the corporate
communications and file sharing backbone for IBM
.*----------------------------
.foilcont
.b As a result, VM and CMS was the host platform for many IBM
innovations:
.d :hp2.Generalized Markup Language (GML):ehp2., a set of markup tags
that are the direct ancestor to XML
.d :hp2.IBM System R:ehp2., the first implementation of SQL
.d :hp2.LEXX:ehp2., the first text editor to use live parsing and
colored syntax highlighting
.d :hp2.PROFS:ehp2., the first commercially-successful office
automation software
.d :hp2.Rexx:ehp2., a high-level programming language considered a
precursor to Tcl and Python
.d The first RISC compiler and linker were written on YMS, a research
operating system running as a VM/370 guest
.*----------------------------
.foil The CCLVM1 VM Configuration
.b CCLVM1 is running :hp2.VM/ESA 2.4.0:ehp2., a version of VM from 1999
.b This can be considered the last version of "old world" VM
.d In December of 1999, IBM would formally add S/390 support to the
Linux kernel.  Linux would eventually become the dominant guest
operating system on VM and use of VM/CMS as an end-user timesharing
environment would greatly diminish.
.d In 2000 the product would be renamed z/VM with the introduction of
64-bit IBM z/Architecture support
.b Rationale for running VM/ESA 2.4.0 on the IBM 3006:
.d Current during the period in which the S/390 Integrated Server was a
supported product
.d Improved reliability over VM/ESA 2.3.0
.d S/390 processor in the 3006 uses 31-bit addressing, so no need for
the z/Architecture support provided by z/VM 3.1
.*----------------------------
.foil Software on CCLVM1
.b Great pains have been taken to outfit CCLVM1 with a wide array of
software appropriate for a 1999-era VM/CMS timesharing system:
.table 4 24 56
.entry Compilers          ¬Available for BASIC, COBOL, Fortran,
.entry                    ¬PL/I, and Rexx
.rule
.entry DCF                ¬Document Composition Facility, used
.entry                    ¬for markdown-based document publishing
.rule
.entry Demos and Games    ¬Curated by current and former IBMers, in
.entry                    ¬addition to examples found in internet
.entry                    ¬archives such as "the CBT tape"
.rule
.entry OfficeVision/VM    ¬Integrated office suite with mail,
.entry                    ¬calendaring, and other features.
.entry                    ¬Successor to PROFS.
.rule
.entry TOOLS              ¬Filesharing and bulletin board system
.entry
.tablend
.*----------------------------
.foil CCLMENU and the Guest Environment
.b We developed a new CMS application called :hp2.CCLMENU:ehp2. to
help visitors to the Classical Computing Laboratory get the most out of
CCLVM1
.b Design philosophy
.d Users with no prior VM or CMS experience should be able to
accomplish meaningful tasks with minimal assistance
.d UX should be visually appealing and show off the qualities of IBM
3270-series terminals
.d The application should take advantage of user libraries developed by
earlier generations of IBMers ("standing on the shoulders of giants")
.*----------------------------
.foilcont
.b To that end, we used two libraries:
.n :hp2.Display Input/Output Facility v.3 (DIOF-3):ehp2., also known as
"IOS3270", is a library that allows CMS Rexx programmers to write
full-screen panel applications without manipulating the 3270 datastream
themselves
.n :hp2.NEATWIND:ehp2. creates and manages overlapping pop-up windows
and dialog boxes, with the ability to use graphical icons on terminals
that support it (such as our 3279-S3G)
.bb In this manner, we guide the user through a curated selection of
CMS applications without them needing to know how to use CMS itself
.d Application panels ask simple numeric response or yes-no questions
.*----------------------------
.foil CCLMENU Features
.n Create a Banner, Sign, or Calendar
.d Pop-up windows guide users through the process of designing a
multi-page vertical banner or a single-page sign.  An IBM VS FORTRAN
version of the famous Snoopy calendar generator can also be used to
create calendars for arbitrary month/year ranges
.n Play a Game
.d A selection of over fifteen games is presented, taken from sources
such as :hp3.Creative Computing:ehp3. magazine, David H. Ahl
programming books, the CBT tape archive, and the personal collections
of IBM retirees
.n View Cool Demos
.d Similar to the above, but with interesting demos
.n Talk with our Advanced AI Chatbot
.d A port of the :hp3.Creative Computing:ehp3. version of ELIZA, with
the option to print transcripts
.*----------------------------
